WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.940
The land of voodoo, the birthplace of zombies,&nbsp;
the cursed land guarded by Baron Samedi, the Lwa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:16.200
or spirit of death. According to tradition,&nbsp;
all this and more can be found in Haiti.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.120
A Caribbean land populated by demons capable&nbsp;
of taking away the will of many Haitians.&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.280
Well VisualPolitik viewers, this at least is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.360
many French thought when this place was&nbsp;
still their colony of Santo Domingo. Or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.780
even the Americans themselves when&nbsp;
they invaded the country in 1916.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.860
In fact, did you know that zombies became popular&nbsp;
in the United States during the American invasion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.940
in the early 20th century? So yes, that has been&nbsp;
the main cultural influence of this country.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:43.020
Of course, let's be honest. This macabre&nbsp;
perception of Haiti and its people served&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.320
a very specific purpose: the ability to&nbsp;
dominate a territory that had become the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:50.820
richest colony in the world and which occupied an&nbsp;
absolutely strategic position in the Caribbean.

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.900
Haiti could have become one of the richest&nbsp;
countries in Latin America. However, destiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.680
had prepared a very different path for it, one&nbsp;
that would lead it to become the poorest country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.520
in Latin America, in the northern hemisphere&nbsp;
and one of the poorest on the entire planet.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.500
Beyond voodoo, zombies and spirits, Haiti today&nbsp;
is practically what we could call a failed State.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
Haiti has been in a spiral of political&nbsp;
instability since its independence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.900
1804. In these almost 200 years, very&nbsp;
few governments have remained stable.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.980
In fact, certainly the most stable period of&nbsp;
the country was also the cruelest period. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.520
are talking about the family dictatorship&nbsp;
of Francois Duvalier and, after his death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.720
that of his son Jean-Claude. Between them, they&nbsp;
held power for almost 30 years and established&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.200
one of the most extreme regimes of terror&nbsp;
in history. But that will be the subject of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.420
another video. Don't forget to subscribe&nbsp;
to VisualPolitik so you don't miss it.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.360
The fact is, of course, that all this&nbsp;
instability was not fortuitous. After&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.520
its independence, Haiti inherited a brewing social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.240
tension based on race... A situation that&nbsp;
made violence become a national hallmark.

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.580
Now, however, things are even worse. Violence&nbsp;
has raged out of control and is rampant. Large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:55.440
parts of the country are in the hands of gangs and&nbsp;
famine lurks. That is why the interim president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.920
Ariel Henry, is looking for a way&nbsp;
to curb gang violence. To do so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.220
he is looking to two of his neighbors:&nbsp;
The United States and El Salvador.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.820
The question is, what exactly has caused&nbsp;
Haiti's desolation? How precisely did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.880
the country end up in the hands of gangs&nbsp;
and criminal groups? How does the Haitian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.720
government hope to mitigate the violence?&nbsp;
What capacity does it have to do so?

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.260
Today we are going to answer these questions,&nbsp;
but first, let's first look at a bit of history.
 &nbsp;

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.260
(ETERNALLY PUNISHED THROUGHOUT HISTORY)

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.280
I don't think it will come as a surprise&nbsp;
to anyone if I say that among the poorest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:35.940
countries in the world we find places&nbsp;
like Burundi, Somalia or Sierra Leone.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:41.160
But what happens if we focus exclusively on the&nbsp;
American continent? This is exactly what happens.&nbsp;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:45.120
Yes, many of you might think that Venezuela&nbsp;
and its Bolivarian revolution made it the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.320
poorest country in the Americas... However,&nbsp;
as you can see, Haiti is still "ahead".

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:53.520
You see, things got off to a bad start very&nbsp;
early on. After its independence from France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:57.360
the European power began a harsh blockade&nbsp;
against its former colony and demanded the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.620
payment of 150 million francs. This, converted&nbsp;
into dollars and adjusted for inflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.960
would amount to more than $700 million dollars.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.220
Of course, that was not the worst. Nor was the&nbsp;
merely extractive occupation of resources that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:17.520
United States carried out between 1916 and 1934.
Worse was the very dynamics of this country.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.880
Tensions between the black and mulatto&nbsp;
populations grew ever more intense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.720
power struggles were constant, coups d'état&nbsp;
followed one after another and presidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.340
assassinations were not uncommon. All washed&nbsp;
down, of course, with plenty of corruption.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.340
So you could say that stability has&nbsp;
been conspicuous by its absence.

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.780
Without going any further, Jovenel Moise,&nbsp;
the president who was assassinated in 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:40.560
was also implicated in corruption cases.&nbsp;
It is something like the prevailing norm.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.500
(Thousands protest against corruption in Haiti&nbsp;

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:49.260
Protesters are demanding President Jovenel Moise&nbsp;
resign for failing to tackle corruption. Months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.380
of protests have engulfed the Western&nbsp;
Hemisphere's poorest country. – DW)

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:56.220
Yet, the most striking feature of Haiti&nbsp;
throughout its 200 years of independence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.220 --> 00:04:00.300
and particularly in the last six&nbsp;
decades, has been violence. Take a look.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:08.520
In 1958, the Haitian dictator Francois&nbsp;
Duvalier created the "Tonton Macoutes",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.000
a militia whose objective was to increase&nbsp;
the tyrant's control over the country and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.120
reduce the presence and influence of the&nbsp;
army. An army that had been trained by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.840
the United States during its occupation&nbsp;
and was not particularly loyal to him.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:22.500
That is to say, the dictator created a sort&nbsp;
of praetorian guard at his personal service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.100
to control the streets. And when his son&nbsp;
Jean-Calude Duvalier was finally overthrown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.480
and this militia was disbanded, suddenly all kinds&nbsp;
of gangs, militias and paramilitary groups began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.680
to proliferate. Let's just say that the members&nbsp;
of the "Tonton Macoutes" quickly found new jobs.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:39.180
Things got even worse when in 1995&nbsp;
President Jean-Bertrand Aristide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:42.960
who had been overthrown by the army&nbsp;
in 1991 and returned to power in 1994&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.380
with the help of the United States,&nbsp;
decided to dissolve the armed forces.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:53.040
And do you know what? Like the former&nbsp;
members of the "Tonton Macoutes",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.760
many soldiers also sought new employment&nbsp;
in the sector they knew best. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:00.060
they joined gangs and guerrilla&nbsp;
groups or created them directly.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.720
These were gangs that began to collaborate&nbsp;
with the various political factions. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.760
President Aristide himself – the one who disbanded&nbsp;
the army – had his own guerrillas, the "chimeras",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.480
who were in charge of attacking the opposition&nbsp;
and silencing dissidence. And so it was until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.760
2004 when he was overthrown again, this time by&nbsp;
a gang known as the "Cannibal Army" along with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:21.420
the paramilitary group "Revolutionary Front&nbsp;
for the Advancement and Progress of Haiti”.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:25.800
And that, VisualPolitik viewers, is how Haiti&nbsp;
came to resemble the boardgame Risk for gangs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.100
paramilitaries and criminal groups.&nbsp;
They took turns imposing their laws.
 &nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.860
And not surprisingly, between power&nbsp;
struggles, social confrontations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.720
corruption and generalized violence, we cannot&nbsp;
be surprised to find news stories like this:

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:40.800
(UN warns of "catastrophic" levels&nbsp;
of hunger in parts of Haiti. – DW)

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.160
In a country of barely 12 million&nbsp;
people, almost 5 million are on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.100
the verge of starvation, and more&nbsp;
than half of them are children.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:50.460
In addition to this we must add the cholera&nbsp;
epidemic that is sweeping the country and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:55.440
that has already amassed at least 15,000&nbsp;
cases and left 450 dead. As far as we know.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:57.720
The situation is critical.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.400
And when critical situations arise,&nbsp;
desperate measures are taken. Listen up.
 &nbsp;

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:13.920
(A DESPERATE PRESIDENT)
 
As I have already said, Haiti's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:18.360
extreme violence is not exactly new. However, what&nbsp;
I have not yet told you is that since 2021, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:22.380
levels of violence and criminality have reached&nbsp;
levels not seen since the Duvalier dictatorship.

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:26.820
And the question is, why? Well, basically,&nbsp;
because of the assassination of Jovenel Moise.&nbsp;

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.660
President Moise was implicated in a corruption&nbsp;
scandal related to the embezzlement of funds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:37.620
from Petrocaribe, the Venezuelan-sponsored&nbsp;
project to sell its oil in the region.

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.200
However, his presidency was&nbsp;
not only marked by corruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.740
but also by perpetuating himself in power&nbsp;
without calling elections when they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:48.000
due and remaining in office after&nbsp;
his term ended on 7 February 2021.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.600
In the end, in July of that same&nbsp;
year he was assassinated by a militia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.860
composed mainly of Colombians. Of course, the&nbsp;
assassination remains a mystery to this day:

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:00.300
Two of the assassins were DEA informants&nbsp;
and several had been trained by the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:04.260
military. Several hypotheses have been put&nbsp;
forward, but the most widely held is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:07.980
Moise was killed just before publishing&nbsp;
a list of drug traffickers to be pursued.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:13.800
Whatever the case, the fact is that Moise&nbsp;
was overthrown, and as always happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.800
the power vacuum is quickly filled&nbsp;
by other players. And this is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.160
one of the great protagonists of&nbsp;
this video appears: Ariel Henry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.580
Haiti's interim president, Prime&nbsp;
Minister and Minister of the Interior.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:31.260
And believe me, since his inauguration,&nbsp;
Henry has had enormous opposition. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:32.700
neurosurgeon and now interim president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:37.020
he is tremendously unpopular. A large part of&nbsp;
Haitians see him as an illegitimate president.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.560
And not only because he has succeeded a&nbsp;
presidency whose term had already expired,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:42.540
but also because he has refused to hold elections,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.140
even though he promised that by the end&nbsp;
of 2022 there would be a date set for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:49.920
the elections. However, he then argued that&nbsp;
the necessary conditions had not been met.

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:51.720
So, far from the elections being called,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.280
after all the deadlines have expired,&nbsp;
we find ourselves with news like this:

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:01.200
(Haiti left with no elected government&nbsp;
officials as it spirals towards anarchy.&nbsp;

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.100
Last 10 remaining senators leave office,&nbsp;
with gangs controlling much of the capital,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:08.100
a malnutrition crisis and a&nbsp;
cholera outbreak. – The Guardian)

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:11.580
In other words, in addition to the fact that&nbsp;
Henry has refused to leave the presidency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.200
now there are no longer any deputies or&nbsp;
senators because their terms have expired.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.700
To give you an idea, even the gangs are demanding&nbsp;
elections. But since Henry has decided to turn a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:26.220
deaf ear... Violence has skyrocketed even more.&nbsp;
For example, in the four years between 2018 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:31.440
2022 alone, the number of homicides practically&nbsp;
tripled to touch the barrier of 20 homicides per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:35.760
hundred thousand inhabitants, making Haiti one&nbsp;
of the most dangerous countries in the world.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:39.540
The gangs have taken to the streets to achieve&nbsp;
what their political partners cannot at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:43.800
ballot box: power over much of the country and&nbsp;
especially much of the capital, Port-au-Prince.&nbsp;

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:52.020
Supported by narcotics and arms trafficking, the&nbsp;
gangs, especially those linked to the coalition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:56.100
of gangs known as G9, have managed, as you&nbsp;
can see on the map, to take control of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:59.820
large part of the coast of the Port-au-Prince&nbsp;
Bay, the nerve center of the Haitian economy.

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:04.380
In fact, to give you an idea, the G9 has even&nbsp;
taken over the country's main oil terminal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:07.140
the Varreux terminal, which&nbsp;
threw Haiti into total chaos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:11.820
since oil is the main source of energy.
To put all this in perspective, let's recap:

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:16.320
We have a country taken over by gangs, with&nbsp;
sky-high violence rates, a lot of corruption,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:19.260
a cholera outbreak, the lowest&nbsp;
per capita income in the Americas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:22.080
no institutions and a president&nbsp;
who does not want to leave power.

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.440
In other words, we have all the possible&nbsp;
ingredients to bring the country to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:29.640
brink of collapse. And that is precisely&nbsp;
the situation in which Haiti finds itself.

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.880
And that has led President Ariel&nbsp;
Henry to make this decision:
 &nbsp;

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.720
(Haitian government asks for&nbsp;
international military assistance. – CNN)

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:39.840
Exactly, VisualPolitik viewers,&nbsp;
the Haitian president himself has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.680
called for other countries to intervene in the&nbsp;
country, particularly the United States. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:46.920
the question is, who on earth would be&nbsp;
willing to carry out such an operation?

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:50.040
That is, to deploy troops to fight&nbsp;
gangs and drug trafficking in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:53.280
completely broken country. Do you&nbsp;
really think this is even possible?

00:09:53.280 --> 00:10:01.560
Well, the truth? It seems unlikely. In fact,&nbsp;
the United States does not want, under any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:06.300
circumstances, to have to send troops again, as&nbsp;
it did in 1916 and 1994, achieving practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:11.160
nothing except to put authoritarian governments&nbsp;
in power that ended up making things even worse.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:16.140
So Washington ducked the issue and passed the&nbsp;
buck to Canada. Trudeau quickly refused. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:20.040
said that Canada would not organize such an&nbsp;
intervention unless there was unanimity among&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:23.820
all political actors in the country. As you&nbsp;
can imagine, that is practically impossible.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:28.200
So the question is, what then? Maybe the UN?&nbsp;
Well, that doesn't look likely either. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:32.460
it is only relatively recently that the UN&nbsp;
ended its Stabilization Mission in Haiti. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:36.840
apart from a more than obvious failure, to top it&nbsp;
all off, the intervention of the blue helmets was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:40.080
fraught with controversy for having apparently&nbsp;
provoked several rapes of Haitian women,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:43.380
covert murders, the introduction of&nbsp;
cholera in the country, etcetera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.380 --> 00:10:47.520
etcetera, etcetera. It is almost certain that&nbsp;
this time the UN will prefer to stay out of it.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.480
Therefore, what the United Nations has proposed is&nbsp;
that this intervention be carried out by a country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.480 --> 00:10:55.560
that I’m pretty sure many of you did not expect to&nbsp;
see in this story: I am talking about the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:57.960
Mexican States... Mexico!
 
And why Mexico?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.880
Basically because of the country's experience in&nbsp;
the fight against organized crime. And not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:09.900
that, it would also alleviate the arrival&nbsp;
of Haitian refugees on its own shores.&nbsp;

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:13.320
Be that as it may, for the moment this&nbsp;
option is stalled. Something like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:17.940
"We have enough problems as it is!”
 
So then... Is Haiti destined to be taken over by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:21.780
gangs? Will the country end up exploding? Well...&nbsp;
For the moment what we know is that the president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:25.920
has taken notice of another security strategy.
 
A strategy that has been making headlines in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.880
international press. I'm certain many of you&nbsp;
already have an idea... Let’s take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:36.960
(BUKELE'S PLAN FOR HAITI?)

00:11:40.200 --> 00:11:44.340
Indeed, today we have to talk once&nbsp;
again about the controversial Bukele,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:47.460
the architect of the destruction of the&nbsp;
balance between security and freedom in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:50.760
El Salvador, but for many, also the&nbsp;
messiah of peace in Latin America.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:54.600
We have already told you both on VisualPolitik&nbsp;
and in our weekly Patreon bulletin, about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:58.620
Bukele is doing in El Salvador. But in case&nbsp;
anyone is lost, I’ll summarize it for you.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:04.680
Overall, the Salvadoran president has&nbsp;
implemented an extremely heavy-handed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.620
strategy against the gangs and&nbsp;
crime plaguing the country. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:11.700
has militarized public security and provided&nbsp;
better equipment to both military and police.

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:16.080
In addition, in order to carry out its drastic&nbsp;
measures, the strategy decreed a State of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:20.700
emergency that has allowed it to suspend several&nbsp;
fundamental rights such as the inviolability of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:24.900
correspondence, privacy of communications, the&nbsp;
right to defense and freedom of association.

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:27.000
A measure that has also allowed the government to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.960
detain gang members and suspected gang&nbsp;
members without the need for a warrant.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:35.220
It has also adopted controversial measures&nbsp;
such as prison sentences of up to 15 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.540
for graffiti alluding to gangs, the reduction of&nbsp;
food and confiscation of mattresses in prisons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:42.960
and the imposition of sanctions on media&nbsp;
outlets that publish gang messages.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:48.660
In other words, it has blatantly&nbsp;
ignored the protection of individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:51.480
rights and liberties. However,&nbsp;
at the cost of less freedom and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.480 --> 00:12:55.620
fewer guarantees... the extreme measures&nbsp;
have increased security in the country.

00:12:55.620 --> 00:13:01.740
Look at this.
 
As you can see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:05.460
the annual homicide figure in El&nbsp;
Salvador has been falling since 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.460 --> 00:13:09.480
but it is undeniable that Bukele has achieved&nbsp;
radically lower numbers... Even though that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:13.200
has come at the cost of such significant issues&nbsp;
as legal security, the presumption of innocence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:17.280
and due process. And then it remains to be seen&nbsp;
what will happen with the big drug traffickers.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:20.100
But it's clear that all these things&nbsp;
in places like Haiti don't seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:22.080
matter much to them either because&nbsp;
they don't really exist right now.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.980
Therefore, it is not surprising that Bukele's&nbsp;
strategy has been considered a success and now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:29.040
the government is considering trying&nbsp;
to replicate it in the island nation.
 &nbsp;

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:32.100
(Haiti to mobilize armed forces&nbsp;
against gangs. – TeleSur)

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:36.780
Now, do you think Ariel Henry would be able&nbsp;
to do the same as Bukele in El Salvador?

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:41.160
Of course, in the country right now there are&nbsp;
hardly any freedoms or rights to lose, the gangs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:45.720
are constantly exercising their violence through&nbsp;
rape, looting, murder, kidnapping, extortion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:49.500
the like. On that front, the proposed extreme&nbsp;
measures do not seem that much of a problem.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.900
But what about Haiti's real capacities? That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:55.380
once it has re-established its army, its&nbsp;
police, and its penitentiary capacity.

00:13:55.380 --> 00:13:58.320
Well, VisualPolitik viewers, the&nbsp;
truth is that with the data in hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.320 --> 00:14:02.460
it seems unlikely for the Haitian&nbsp;
government to pull off a Bukele.&nbsp;

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:09.960
As you can see, Haiti, a country with&nbsp;
almost twice the population of El Salvador,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:14.160
has less than half the number of police officers,&nbsp;
a miniature army and very little prison capacity.&nbsp;

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:16.800
Especially after the construction&nbsp;
of the mega-prison in El Salvador,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:19.440
which will be able to hold up&nbsp;
to 40,000 additional prisoners.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:23.400
The curious thing about all this, by&nbsp;
the way, is that between 2019 and 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:28.680
Haiti received about $2.5 billion in international&nbsp;
aid which as you can see has not been put to good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:32.340
use. Keep in mind that Haiti is, according&nbsp;
to Transparency International, one of the 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:35.820
most corrupt countries on the entire planet. So&nbsp;
there doesn't seem to be much hope there either.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:38.640
In other words, Haiti's future&nbsp;
does not look good. Surely now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:41.580
you understand better why this is&nbsp;
the poorest country in the Americas.

